    "### 📄 RC — Living with intermittency",
    "",
    "Solar panels generate no electricity at night, and wind turbines fall idle on a still day, a fact that critics cite as renewable energy's fatal weakness. Yet a national grid is not one solar panel or one turbine; it is thousands of them spread across mountains, coastlines, and deserts, and the odds that the entire sun and wind fall silent everywhere at once are vanishingly small. Add a diverse mix of hydropower, which can be stored behind a dam and released on demand, and the picture of a fragile, all-or-nothing supply starts to look overstated.",
    "",
    "Battery storage, meanwhile, is falling in cost as fast as solar panels did a decade ago, letting grids bank cheap daytime sunlight for use after dark. None of this means intermittency is a myth; grids built mostly on wind and sun do need more transmission lines and more storage than a grid built on a single always-on coal plant.",
    "",
    "The honest claim is narrower than either side's slogan: intermittency is a real engineering problem, solved by diversity and storage, not a reason to abandon renewable power altogether.",
