    "### 📄 RC — The architecture of a habit",
    "",
    "A habit, psychologists note, is not simply something we do often; it is something we do with almost no conscious deliberation, triggered automatically by a cue in our surroundings. The cue might be a location, a time of day, or an emotional state, and once a behaviour has been repeated enough times in response to that cue, the brain begins to run it with the mental effort of turning a doorknob rather than the effort of solving a puzzle.",
    "",
    "This is why habits are so hard to break by willpower alone: willpower is a limited, effortful resource, while a habit, once formed, bypasses effort entirely. The more useful strategy, researchers argue, is to change the cue rather than fight the response — moving snacks out of sight, for instance, removes the trigger instead of demanding constant refusal.",
    "",
    "Equally, new habits form fastest when attached to an existing, reliable cue: doing ten push-ups immediately after brushing one's teeth borrows the automaticity already built around the toothbrush. Habit change, on this view, is less a battle of self-control and more a redesign of one's environment.",
    "",
    "**Q25.** Which of the following best states the central idea of the passage?",
    "A) Willpower alone is the only tool against bad habits B) Habits are cue-triggered, best changed by changing the cue C) New habits take longer to form than old ones take to break D) Emotional states are the only trigger for habits",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** The passage defines habits as cue-triggered automatic behaviours, explains why willpower fails against them, and closes by reframing habit change as \"less a battle of self-control and more a redesign of one's environment\" — that is, change the cue. This is option B. Option A is the opposite of the passage's actual recommendation. Option C compares timeframes the passage never measures. Option D wrongly narrows cues to only emotional states, when the passage also lists location and time of day.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC main-idea question on a behavioural-science passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q26.** According to the passage, why are habits difficult to break through willpower alone?",
    "A) Willpower is limited, but a habit needs no real effort B) Habits are stronger than any human emotion C) Willpower only works on new habits, never old ones D) Cues can never be identified or changed by anyone",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage states this exactly: \"willpower is a limited, effortful resource, while a habit, once formed, bypasses effort entirely.\" That contrast — limited effortful willpower versus effortless habit — is option A. Option B compares habits to emotions, a comparison the passage never makes. Option C invents a distinction between new and old habits that is not in the text. Option D contradicts the passage's own advice to change cues, which assumes cues can be identified and altered.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC specific-detail/reasoning question.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
