    "### 📄 RC — When coral turns pale",
    "",
    "A coral reef's vivid colour comes not from the coral animal itself but from microscopic algae living inside its tissue, in a partnership that benefits both sides: the algae receive shelter and access to sunlight, while the coral receives sugars that the algae produce through photosynthesis. When ocean water grows unusually warm for a sustained period, this partnership breaks down; the stressed coral expels its algae, and the reef turns a ghostly white, a phenomenon called bleaching.",
    "",
    "A bleached reef is not necessarily dead — corals can recapture algae and recover if temperatures fall again within a few weeks — but repeated or prolonged bleaching starves the coral of its main food source and leaves it vulnerable to disease.",
    "",
    "Because reefs shelter roughly a quarter of all marine species despite covering under one percent of the ocean floor, a bleaching event's damage extends far beyond the coral itself, disrupting fish nurseries and the coastal fisheries that depend on them. Scientists now treat the frequency of bleaching, rather than any single event, as the truest signal of a reef's long-term prospects.",
    "",
    "**Q29.** Which of the following best captures the central idea of the passage?",
    "A) Coral reefs worldwide are already permanently dead B) Bleaching harms the coral-algae partnership widely C) Algae are parasites that harm coral reefs D) Only fisheries are affected when a reef bleaches",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** The passage explains what bleaching is (a breakdown of the coral-algae partnership under heat stress), how it can be survived or worsen, and why it matters beyond the reef itself (a quarter of marine species, fish nurseries, fisheries). Together this is option B. Option A contradicts the passage's own statement that bleached reefs \"can recapture algae and recover\". Option C misreads the relationship, which the passage explicitly calls beneficial to both sides. Option D ignores the passage's point that fish nurseries and biodiversity are affected too, not fisheries alone.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC main-idea question on a marine-science passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q30.** According to the passage, what benefit does the coral receive from the algae living in its tissue?",
    "A) Shelter from ocean predators B) Sugars produced by the algae through photosynthesis C) Protection from unusually warm water D) Its bright colour only, with no nutritional benefit",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** The passage states the exchange directly: the algae get shelter and sunlight, \"while the coral receives sugars that the algae produce through photosynthesis.\" That is option B, precisely as written. Option A reverses the exchange — shelter is what the algae receive, not the coral. Option C is unsupported; warm water is what damages the partnership, not something the coral is protected from. Option D ignores the passage's explicit mention of a nutritional benefit (sugars), not merely colour.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC specific-detail question testing a two-way exchange.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q31.** According to the passage, a bleached reef",
    "A) is always permanently and irreversibly dead B) can recover if cooler temperatures return within a few weeks C) recovers only if the water stays warm for even longer D) never regains its algae under any circumstances",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** The passage says this plainly: \"A bleached reef is not necessarily dead — corals can recapture algae and recover if temperatures fall again within a few weeks.\" That directly matches option B. Option A contradicts this sentence outright. Option C reverses the condition for recovery, which requires cooling, not continued warmth. Option D again contradicts the passage's statement that algae can be recaptured.",
    "",
