    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q32.** Why, according to the passage, do scientists treat the frequency of bleaching as the truest signal of a reef's long-term prospects?",
    "A) A single bleaching event always kills a reef at once B) Repeated bleaching starves coral and raises disease risk C) Bleaching frequency has no effect on coral health D) Only a reef's very first bleaching event matters",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** The passage links repetition directly to lasting damage: \"repeated or prolonged bleaching starves the coral of its main food source and leaves it vulnerable to disease.\" A reef that bleaches often is repeatedly starved and repeatedly weakened, which is why frequency, not any one event, best predicts its future — option B. Option A is contradicted by the earlier statement that a single bleaching event is often survivable. Option C denies a link the passage clearly draws. Option D invents a special status for the \"first\" event that the passage never mentions.",
