    "**Q41.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. Regular moderate exercise, studies consistently show, reduces symptoms of mild depression about as effectively as some medications, without the side effects drugs can carry. The mechanism is not fully settled — candidates include improved blood flow to the brain, the release of mood-lifting chemicals, and simply the confidence gained from mastering a physical challenge. What is settled is the dose: as little as thirty minutes of brisk walking most days produces a measurable lift, and more intense exercise adds only modest further benefit.",
    "A) Moderate exercise helps mood; modest amounts already work B) Exercise cures depression better than any medication C) Scientists have fully explained why exercise helps mood D) Only intense daily workouts give any real mood benefit",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** A good summary must capture all three parts of the passage: exercise measurably helps mood, its exact mechanism is still debated, and even a modest dose (thirty minutes of walking) already works. Option A captures the core claim — meaningful benefit from a modest amount — without overreaching. Option B overstates the passage's careful \"about as effectively as some medications\", turning a comparison into an absolute claim of superiority. Option C contradicts the passage, which says the mechanism \"is not fully settled\". Option D directly contradicts the passage's statement that a small dose already produces a measurable lift.",
