    "**Q42.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. When a city charges drivers a fee to enter its busiest streets during peak hours, two things tend to happen: some drivers shift their trips to off-peak times, and others switch to buses, trains, or bicycles instead. The result is fewer cars competing for the same road space exactly when space is scarcest. Revenue from the fee, several cities have found, can then be reinvested in the very public transport that absorbs the diverted trips, making the switch easier for the next commuter who is priced off the road.",
