    "**Q43.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. Decades of aggressively extinguishing every forest fire, however small, left many forests choked with dry underbrush that would once have been cleared by frequent, low-intensity burns. When a fire eventually does break out in such a forest, it has far more fuel to consume, and it burns hotter and spreads faster than the smaller fires nature once used to keep the underbrush in check. Ironically, then, decades of fire suppression can make the eventual, unavoidable fire more destructive rather than less.",
