    "**Q46.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. Every time an antibiotic kills most of a bacterial population but not all of it, the few survivors are disproportionately those that happened to carry some trait resisting the drug. Left to reproduce without competition from their killed neighbours, these survivors pass their resistance on, and the next generation of bacteria is harder to treat. Overusing antibiotics — for infections that would clear on their own, or by stopping a course early — simply repeats this filtering process more often, accelerating the rise of drug-resistant bacteria.",
