    "## 🟡 Medium — the scoring zone",
    "",
    "### 📄 RC — The residue of attention",
    "",
    "For decades, the word \"multitasking\" has been used as a badge of pride in offices and classrooms alike, as though the brain were a computer that could run several programs at once without any of them slowing down. Cognitive scientists have found otherwise. When a person switches from one task to another — say, from writing an email to answering a phone call — the brain does not cleanly close the first task before opening the second. A trace of the abandoned task, which researchers call \"attention residue\", lingers and competes for mental resources even after the switch has occurred.",
    "",
    "This residue is why a meeting attended while also drafting a report often produces a worse report and a poorer grasp of the meeting than either task tackled alone. The effect is strongest when the interrupted task is left unfinished, since an open loop is harder for the mind to set aside than a closed one. None of this means switching tasks is always foolish; short, low-stakes interruptions leave little residue behind. But it does mean that the person proudly juggling five demanding tasks at once is very likely getting less done, not more, than they imagine.",
    "",
    "**Q63.** Which of the following best captures the central idea of the passage?",
    "A) Multitasking always produces more thorough work B) Task-switching leaves residue that hurts performance C) Meetings must never be combined with any other task D) Short interruptions cost more than long ones do",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** Let us walk through this slowly. The passage's whole point is that when you switch from one task to another, a small trace of the first task, called \"attention residue\", stays behind in your mind and competes for your attention. Because of this residue, the second task (and often the first task too) gets done worse than if you had done them one at a time. That is exactly what option B says, so B is correct. Option A says the opposite of what the passage argues — the passage says switching tasks usually makes things worse, not better, so A is wrong. Option C is too extreme: the passage gives an example about meetings, but it never says meetings must \"never\" be combined with anything else — that word \"never\" is stronger than anything the passage claims. Option D is backwards: the passage says short, low-stakes interruptions leave very little residue, which means they usually cost less, not more, than long interruptions.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC main-idea question requiring you to state the passage's core claim precisely.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q64.** The author opens with the analogy of a computer running several programs mainly in order to",
    "A) prove computers multitask better than human brains B) introduce a common but mistaken view of the mind C) explain exactly how attention residue is measured D) argue that all offices should ban multitasking",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** Read the very next sentence after the computer analogy: \"Cognitive scientists have found otherwise.\" The word \"otherwise\" tells us the computer comparison was a popular belief that the rest of the passage is about to correct. So the analogy's job is simply to set up a common, mistaken assumption before knocking it down — that is option B. Option A is wrong because the passage never actually compares how well computers multitask versus brains; it only borrows the computer idea as a stand-in for a mistaken belief. Option C is wrong because no measurement method is ever described in the passage. Option D is wrong because the passage never recommends any office policy at all — it only describes a mental effect.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC function-of-example/analogy question inside a cognitive-science passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q65.** According to the passage, all of the following are true EXCEPT:",
    "A) Residue can persist after a task switch B) Unfinished tasks leave more residue than finished ones C) All interruptions leave equal residue, whatever length D) Two demanding tasks done together degrade both",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** For an EXCEPT question, three options will be clearly supported by the passage, and one will not be — your job is to find the one unsupported option. Option A is supported directly: the passage says residue \"lingers\" after a switch. Option B is supported: the passage says the effect is strongest when a task \"is left unfinished\". Option D is supported: the meeting-and-report example shows both tasks coming out worse. Option C, however, directly contradicts the passage, which says \"short, low-stakes interruptions leave little residue behind\" — that is the opposite of saying all interruptions leave equal residue no matter how long or short they are. So C is the one statement the passage does NOT support, making it correct for this EXCEPT question.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC EXCEPT-style detail question demanding careful line-by-line matching.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q66.** Which situation best illustrates \"attention residue\" as the passage describes it?",
    "A) A chef takes a call, returns still thinking of it B) A commuter enjoys music while driving, fully focused C) A student finishes one task, then starts another D) An office bans phones during all of its meetings",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Attention residue, as the passage defines it, is a trace of an earlier task that lingers in the mind and competes for attention after you have already switched to a new task. Option A matches this exactly: the chef switches from chopping to a call, and afterward part of their mind is still on the call even though they have returned to chopping — that lingering trace is the residue itself. Option B is wrong because no task-switch or leftover distraction is described at all; the commuter stays fully focused. Option C is wrong for a sharper reason: the passage says a finished, \"closed\" task leaves little residue, so a student closing a completed assignment is actually the example of residue being absent, not present. Option D just describes a rule an office adopts, not an instance of the mental effect itself.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC application question asking you to spot the concept in a brand-new scenario.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
