    "**Q86.** The five sentences below, labelled 1 to 5, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. The resulting scores, though numerically comparable, therefore measure something closer to a family's resources than to any single, universal standard of ability. 2. Standardized tests promise a single, comparable yardstick, so that a student's score means roughly the same thing regardless of which school, city or year produced it. 3. None of this means standardized tests should be scrapped outright, only that the \"single yardstick\" they promise is more fragile than it first appears. 4. In practice, wealthier schools often provide dedicated coaching, smaller classes and past papers that poorer schools cannot afford, quietly breaking that condition. 5. That promise depends on one condition holding true: students across schools must have had a genuinely comparable chance to prepare for the test in the first place. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 2-5-4-1-3 B) 2-4-5-1-3 C) 5-2-4-1-3 D) 2-5-1-4-3",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Sentence 2 introduces the promise of standardized tests: a single, comparable yardstick. Sentence 5 begins \"That promise depends on one condition\", directly referring back to the promise in sentence 2, so 2 is followed by 5. Sentence 4 then describes how, \"in practice\", wealthier schools break that very condition sentence 5 just introduced, so 5 is followed by 4. Sentence 1 begins \"The resulting scores... therefore\", drawing the direct consequence of the broken condition in sentence 4, so 4 is followed by 1. Sentence 3 opens with \"None of this\", a summary phrase that only makes sense once the whole chain — promise, condition, breakage, consequence — has already been laid out, so it comes last. The order is 2-5-4-1-3, option A. Option B swaps sentences 4 and 5, but then sentence 4's \"that condition\" would have no earlier sentence defining what the condition actually is. Option C opens with sentence 5, but \"that promise\" at the very start has nothing to point back to yet. Option D moves sentence 1 before sentence 4, but sentence 1's \"resulting scores... therefore\" needs the specific unfair mechanism in sentence 4 to already be described as its cause.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-jumble (TITA) on standardized testing, cause-effect anchor chain.*",
