    "**Q87.** The five sentences below, labelled 1 to 5, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. Whales attempting to call across an ocean basin must now compete with this din, and some populations have been recorded shortening their calls or repeating them more often, much as a person raises their voice at a noisy party. 2. A quieter ocean, then, is not simply a pleasant idea, but arguably a precondition for animals whose entire way of navigating the world depends on being heard. 3. Sound travels roughly four times faster and much farther underwater than it does through air, which is precisely why many marine animals rely on sound rather than sight to find food, mates and each other across vast distances. 4. Such compensations are not free: singing louder or longer costs an animal energy it would otherwise spend on other things, like finding food. 5. In recent decades, however, commercial shipping, oil exploration and sonar have added a near-constant hum of engine noise and sharp acoustic blasts to large stretches of ocean that were once far quieter. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 3-5-1-4-2 B) 3-1-5-4-2 C) 5-3-1-4-2 D) 3-5-4-1-2",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Sentence 3 sets up why sound matters so much to marine animals in the first place; this general background must open the paragraph. Sentence 5 begins with \"however\", a contrast word that needs sentence 3's point (sound is vital underwater) to contrast against with new human noise, so 3 is followed by 5. Sentence 1 begins \"Whales... must now compete with this din\" — \"this din\" points directly at the noise just introduced in sentence 5, so 5 is followed by 1. Sentence 4 begins \"Such compensations\", referring to the shortened or repeated calls described in sentence 1, so 1 is followed by 4. Sentence 2 concludes with \"then\", summing up the whole chain, so it comes last. The order is 3-5-1-4-2, option A. Option B moves sentence 1 before sentence 5, but \"this din\" in sentence 1 has no earlier sentence introducing any noise to refer to yet. Option C opens with sentence 5, but its opening word \"however\" has nothing before it to contrast against. Option D moves sentence 4 before sentence 1, but \"such compensations\" in sentence 4 needs the specific compensating behaviours (shortened, repeated calls) from sentence 1 to already be described.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-jumble (TITA) on ocean-noise ecology, contrast-word anchor.*",
