    "**Q89.** The five sentences below, labelled 1 to 5, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. Those survivors can then multiply largely unopposed, passing their resistance on to their offspring and, in some cases, to entirely different species of bacteria nearby. 2. Every course of antibiotics a patient takes kills the bacteria most vulnerable to the drug first, leaving behind, by definition, whichever bacteria happened to be hardiest against it. 3. The advice to \"finish the course\" is therefore not bureaucratic caution but a direct attempt to deny survivors of the first round any chance to multiply and spread. 4. Over enough patients and enough incomplete courses, a hospital or even an entire region can end up with bacterial populations that no longer respond reliably to a once-effective drug. 5. If the course is stopped early, once the patient merely feels better rather than fully recovered, an unusually large share of those hardier survivors remains alive inside the body. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 2-5-1-4-3 B) 2-1-5-4-3 C) 5-2-1-4-3 D) 2-5-4-1-3",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Sentence 2 sets up the general mechanism: any course of antibiotics leaves behind the hardiest bacteria. Sentence 5 begins \"If the course is stopped early\", using the definite phrase \"the course\" that needs sentence 2's course to already be on the table, so 2 is followed by 5. Sentence 1 begins \"Those survivors can then multiply\", and \"those survivors\" means the unusually large group of hardy survivors left behind specifically because the course was stopped early, as sentence 5 just described, so 5 is followed by 1. Sentence 4 begins \"Over enough patients\", scaling up the individual-level spread from sentence 1 into a hospital- or region-wide problem, so 1 is followed by 4. Sentence 3 concludes with \"therefore\", drawing the practical lesson from the whole chain, so it comes last. The order is 2-5-1-4-3, option A. Option B moves sentence 1 before sentence 5, but \"those survivors\" in sentence 1 is meant to mean the unusually large surviving group created by stopping the course early, a point only made in sentence 5. Option C opens with sentence 5, but \"the course\" has no earlier sentence establishing what course is being discussed. Option D moves sentence 4 before sentence 1, but \"over enough patients\" needs the individual spreading mechanism in sentence 1 to already be described before it can be scaled up.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-jumble (TITA) on antibiotic resistance, definite-article anchor.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
