    "**Q97.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. Look closely at almost any steel bridge truss (a framework of connected beams that supports the load) and you will find it built from triangles rather than squares or rectangles. The reason is structural, not decorative. A square frame with hinged corners can be pushed sideways into a slanted parallelogram without changing the length of any of its four sides, meaning the shape itself offers no resistance to that sideways force. A triangle, by contrast, cannot be deformed at all without changing the length of at least one of its three sides, so as long as the beams themselves resist stretching and compressing, which steel does well, the triangle's overall shape is locked rigid. Engineers exploit this simple geometric fact by building trusses from repeated triangular units.",
    "A) Triangles resist deforming, so trusses use them B) Square trusses are stronger than triangular ones C) Steel is used in bridges mainly for its appearance D) Triangular trusses need no steel to stay rigid",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's core geometric fact is that a triangle's shape cannot change at all unless one of its three sides actually changes length, while a square can lean into a slanted shape without any side changing length — so triangles alone give a truss shape-based rigidity, which engineers then exploit. That is option A. Option B is the exact opposite of the passage's point: the passage explains why squares can deform sideways easily while triangles cannot, meaning triangles, not squares, are the stronger shape here. Option C is directly contradicted: \"The reason is structural, not decorative.\" Option D is contradicted too, since the triangle's rigidity is explicitly said to depend on the beams themselves resisting stretching and compressing, \"which steel does well\" — remove the steel's strength, and the triangle argument no longer holds.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-summary MCQ on a structural-engineering passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q98.** The passage below is followed by four summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage. Many central banks today announce a specific inflation target, often around two percent a year, and commit publicly to steering monetary policy toward hitting it. The number itself is not sacred; economists have long debated whether two percent, one percent, or some other figure best balances the costs of rising prices against the risks of prices falling too low. What matters more than the exact figure, most agree, is the credibility of the commitment. If households and businesses believe a central bank will actually defend its target, they set wages and prices in line with that expectation, which in turn helps the target become self-fulfilling. A central bank that changes its target frequently, or misses it repeatedly without consequence, loses precisely this credibility, and once lost, it is far harder to steer expectations back into line than it was to set them in the first place.",
    "A) A credible target shapes expectations that fulfil it B) The exact target number matters more than credibility C) Central banks never miss their announced targets D) Households ignore central bank targets when setting wages",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's central claim is stated directly: \"what matters more than the exact figure... is the credibility of the commitment\", because a credible target shapes what households and businesses expect, and that expectation then helps make the target come true — a self-fulfilling loop. That is option A. Option B reverses the passage's own ranking, which explicitly says credibility matters more than the exact number. Option C is contradicted by the passage's own mention of a bank that \"misses it repeatedly without consequence.\" Option D is contradicted by the entire mechanism described: households and businesses DO set wages and prices based on the target, which is precisely why credibility matters so much.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-summary MCQ on a macroeconomics passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
