    "**Q106.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. People frequently lose track of everyday objects like keys or glasses not because their memory is failing in general, but because placing an object down is often done on autopilot, without any real attention paid to the act itself. Memory researchers note that an event must be noticed, even briefly, to be recorded at all; an action performed while the mind is elsewhere is, essentially, filed nowhere. This is why deliberately narrating a mundane action out loud — saying \"keys on the shelf\" while doing it — measurably improves later recall of where the object went. ___",
    "A) The fix is not a better memory but a moment of attention B) Losing objects proves human memory is fundamentally weak C) Only elderly people struggle to recall where objects go D) Narrating actions aloud has no effect on later memory",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Trace the paragraph's own logic to see what conclusion it is building toward. It says the real problem is not weak memory but a lack of attention at the moment an object is placed down, and it gives evidence (narrating an action out loud) showing that adding attention fixes the problem. The natural closing sentence should name this lesson directly: the fix is attention, not better memory — exactly option A. Option B overreaches, since the passage never says memory in general is \"fundamentally unreliable\"; it specifically blames a lack of attention, not memory itself. Option C invents an age-related claim that never appears anywhere in the passage. Option D flatly contradicts the passage's own evidence, which says narrating an action \"measurably improves\" recall, not that it has no effect.",
