    "**Q107.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. Most people, after a small public stumble — tripping on a step, mispronouncing a word in a meeting — feel certain that everyone around noticed and will remember it. Psychologists who study this call it the spotlight effect: the sense that one's own actions are illuminated in other people's minds far more brightly than they actually are. Careful studies bear this out. When researchers asked bystanders afterward what they recalled of a staged, mildly awkward moment, most had noticed far less, and forgotten it far sooner, than the person at the centre of it ever imagined. ___",
    "A) People routinely overestimate how closely others track us B) Bystanders always remember our mistakes better than we do C) Public mistakes permanently damage a person's reputation D) The spotlight effect proves other people do not care",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph's evidence is specific: bystanders noticed and remembered a staged awkward moment far less than the person at its centre imagined. The fitting closing sentence should state the general lesson this evidence supports — that we routinely overestimate how much others notice and remember our own mistakes — exactly option A. Option B directly contradicts the study just described, in which bystanders remembered LESS, not more, than the person who stumbled. Option C reaches for an extreme, permanent consequence that the passage's evidence about quick forgetting does not support at all. Option D takes a specific finding about noticing and memory and stretches it into an unsupported claim about whether people care about us, which the passage never discusses.",
