    "**Q108.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. Give a person a shelf they assembled themselves and one identical shelf a professional installed for them, and, oddly, the self-assembled one is very often valued more highly by its owner, even judged by the same person applying the same standards. Researchers who first identified this pattern, naming it after a furniture retailer famous for flat-pack assembly, found that the effort of building something creates a kind of attachment separate from the object's actual quality or usefulness. The effect fades sharply, however, if the assembly is left obviously unfinished or badly botched, suggesting the extra value comes from completed effort, not effort alone. ___",
    "A) Labour raises value mainly when effort is completed well B) Handmade furniture is always sturdier than factory made C) People value objects only in proportion to money spent D) The effect disappears completely once a task feels hard",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph's final stated nuance is that the extra value people feel toward something they built comes from finishing the effort successfully, not merely from putting in effort of any kind, since the value fades when assembly is left unfinished or botched. The fitting closing sentence should restate this nuance plainly, exactly as option A does. Option B introduces durability, a completely different property from perceived value, which the passage never measures or discusses. Option C wrongly substitutes money spent for the actual variable the passage is about, which is labour or effort, not price. Option D confuses two different things: the passage's fading condition is about a task being left unfinished or botched, not merely about a task feeling difficult, so this option invents a distinct and unsupported claim.",
