    "**Q109.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. The QWERTY keyboard layout was designed in the 1870s, in part to slow typists down and keep mechanical typewriter arms from jamming into each other on fast, adjacent keystrokes. That mechanical constraint disappeared decades ago with the electric typewriter and later the computer, both of which have no jamming arms to protect. Yet QWERTY remains the dominant layout on keyboards worldwide, even though alternative layouts, tested since the 1930s, have shown modest typing-speed advantages for practiced users. The reason has little to do with the layout's own merits: once millions of typists, keyboards and training manuals were built around QWERTY, the cost of everyone switching together came to outweigh the modest gains any individual layout might offer. ___",
    "A) A technology can persist through momentum, not merit B) QWERTY was proven the fastest layout in testing C) Alternative layouts have all been shown worse than QWERTY D) Mechanical jamming still limits typing speed today",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph traces a clear arc: QWERTY's original mechanical reason for existing vanished long ago, yet it survives purely because so many people, keyboards and manuals are already built around it, making a coordinated switch too costly to be worth the modest gains of a better layout. The fitting closing sentence should name this general lesson — that a widely adopted technology can persist through sheer momentum rather than genuine merit — exactly option A. Option B is directly contradicted, since the passage says alternatives showed \"modest typing-speed advantages\", meaning QWERTY was never proven fastest. Option C overstates the passage's claim into an absolute ranking the text never makes. Option D is directly contradicted by the passage's own statement that the mechanical jamming problem \"disappeared decades ago.\"",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-completion question on a technology-history/lock-in passage.*",
