    "**Q110.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. For decades, the pleasant, calm alertness that follows sustained aerobic exercise — the so-called runner's high — was attributed almost entirely to endorphins, the body's natural painkilling chemicals. More recent research complicates that story. Endorphins are large molecules that struggle to cross from the bloodstream into the brain in meaningful amounts, which raises the question of how they could produce a mental effect at all. Attention has shifted to a different set of molecules, endocannabinoids, which are smaller, cross into the brain more readily, and rise reliably in the blood after sustained moderate exercise. ___",
    "A) Endocannabinoids may better explain the mental effect B) Endorphins have now been proven to play no role at all C) Runner's high only occurs during very short sprints D) The brain cannot be affected by chemicals in the blood",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph builds toward a specific shift in scientific attention: from endorphins, which struggle to reach the brain in useful amounts, toward endocannabinoids, which cross into the brain more easily and rise reliably with exercise. The fitting closing sentence should name this shift directly, exactly as option A does, using the appropriately cautious \"may better explain\" rather than an absolute claim. Option B overreaches, since the passage says attention has merely \"shifted\", a complication of the old story, not a proof that endorphins play \"no role at all.\" Option C invents a sprinting detail that contradicts the passage's own description of \"sustained moderate exercise.\" Option D is an absurd overgeneralisation, directly contradicted by the passage's whole point that endocannabinoids do cross into the brain.",
