    "**Q111.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. Food left too long in a home freezer often develops greyish-white, dry-looking patches, a condition known as freezer burn, that most people assume means the food is unsafe to eat. In fact, freezer burn is a moisture problem, not a safety one: over time, water trapped in the food slowly turns to vapour and escapes through tiny gaps in the packaging, leaving that part of the food dehydrated and its texture noticeably tougher or grainier. No harmful bacteria are especially associated with this specific process, since the food has remained frozen throughout, well below the temperature at which most bacteria can grow. ___",
    "A) Freezer burn mainly harms texture, not food safety B) Freezer burn means food is contaminated and unsafe C) Bacteria multiply fastest at freezing temperatures D) Freezer burn happens because freezers are not cold enough",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph corrects a popular misconception step by step: freezer burn is caused by moisture escaping, not by contamination, and no harmful bacteria are specially tied to this process because freezing temperatures keep bacteria from growing well anyway. The fitting closing sentence should sum up this corrected understanding, exactly as option A does. Option B repeats the very misconception the paragraph has just spent its whole length correcting. Option C is directly contradicted, since the paragraph says bacteria cannot grow well \"at the temperature at which most bacteria can grow\" is exceeded only above freezing, meaning bacteria grow poorly, not fastest, at freezing temperatures. Option D invents a cause (an insufficiently cold freezer) that the passage never mentions; the real cause given is moisture escaping through packaging gaps.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — para-completion question on a food-science passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q112.** The paragraph below is incomplete. Choose the sentence that completes it most coherently. A traveller who flies from Mumbai to New York arrives to find the local clock several hours behind the time their body still expects, a mismatch popularly called jet lag. The discomfort does not come from the flight's length alone; travelling the same number of hours north-south, without crossing time zones, produces far milder fatigue. What jet lag really disrupts is the body's internal circadian clock, a roughly 24-hour cycle governing sleepiness, hunger and hormone release, which resets only gradually, typically by about one hour per day, no matter how instantly the calendar and wall clock have jumped. ___",
    "A) Crossing time zones outpaces the body clock's own reset B) Jet lag is caused mainly by the strain of long flights C) The body's circadian clock resets instantly on landing D) Flying the same distance always causes jet lag",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The paragraph's whole explanation builds to one point: the wall clock and calendar can jump instantly across time zones, but the body's own internal clock can only shift by about an hour a day, so the two fall out of step. The fitting closing sentence should state this mismatch in speed directly, exactly as option A does. Option B is directly contradicted, since the passage says the discomfort \"does not come from the flight's length alone\", and a same-length north-south flight produces \"far milder fatigue.\" Option C is contradicted by the passage's own statement that the clock resets \"only gradually.\" Option D is contradicted by the same north-south flight example, which shows that distance alone, without crossing time zones, does not produce the same jet lag.",
