    "**Q120.** Based on the passage, which scenario best exemplifies a \"trespasser in the pejorative sense\"?",
    "A) A chemist states an untested cure with total certainty B) A biologist proposes a testable idea after wide reading C) An economist questions her own field's assumptions D) A historian declines to comment outside her subject",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's own test for the bad kind of trespassing is: no genuine evidence, no tentativeness, and total certainty in a field you do not belong to. The chemist in option A does exactly this: talks about a disease's cure (not chemistry), offers no evidence, and speaks with \"total certainty.\" That fits the pejorative trespasser perfectly. Option B is wrong because the biologist reads widely and proposes something testable, which the passage calls the mark of a genuine contributor, not a trespasser. Option C is wrong because the economist is questioning her OWN field, so there is no crossing of any border at all. Option D is wrong for the same reason — declining to comment outside one's subject is the complete opposite of trespassing.",
