    "### 📄 RC — Credit that compounds",
    "",
    "In 1968, the sociologist Robert Merton named a pattern he had noticed across scientific careers: when a discovery is made by a team including one already-eminent researcher and several less-known collaborators, credit accrues disproportionately to the eminent name, regardless of who actually contributed the crucial insight. He called it the Matthew effect, after the biblical verse promising that to those who have, more will be given.",
    "",
    "The mechanism is not simply vanity or injustice, though both play a part. Eminence functions as a signal under conditions of scarce attention: journal readers, prize committees and hiring panels cannot verify every contributor's actual role, so they fall back on a known name as a proxy for quality, which is often a reasonable shortcut and only sometimes a distorting one. The effect compounds over a career, since early credit brings the resources — funding, favoured students, faster publication — that make future high-profile discoveries more likely, which then earn still more credit. What looks, from outside, like a meritocracy rewarding talent is, on this account, partly a self-reinforcing loop that rewards prior reward. The corrective is not to deny that eminent researchers are often genuinely skilled, but to notice how much of their subsequent advantage was manufactured by the very system meant only to detect it.",
    "",
    "**Q121.** The passage's central claim is best summarised as:",
    "A) Scientific credit partly rewards prior credit itself B) Eminent scientists never deserve the credit they receive C) Robert Merton disproved that talent matters in science D) Prize committees always verify each contributor's role",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's key sentence says the situation \"is, on this account, partly a self-reinforcing loop that rewards prior reward,\" contrasted with what \"looks, from outside, like a meritocracy.\" That is exactly the claim in option A: credit is partly about credit itself, not only about merit. Option B is wrong because the passage explicitly says it does not deny that eminent researchers \"are often genuinely skilled\" — it never says they never deserve credit. Option C is wrong because naming the effect does not disprove that talent matters; the passage only adds that talent is not the whole story. Option D is wrong because the passage says panels \"cannot verify every contributor's actual role\", meaning they do NOT always verify — the opposite of option D.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC central-claim question on a dense sociology-of-science passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q122.** According to the passage, panels rely on a researcher's fame mainly because:",
    "A) they cannot verify each contributor's true role directly B) fame is legally required for prize consideration C) less-known collaborators refuse to be named at all D) eminent researchers always did the crucial work",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage states directly: \"journal readers, prize committees and hiring panels cannot verify every contributor's actual role, so they fall back on a known name as a proxy.\" In plain words, checking each person's real contribution is hard, so panels use fame as a shortcut instead. That is option A. Option B invents a legal rule the passage never mentions. Option C is wrong because the passage never says collaborators refuse to be named; it says credit goes to the eminent name regardless of who deserves it. Option D is wrong because the whole passage argues fame is used \"regardless of who actually contributed the crucial insight\" — meaning the famous person did NOT always do the crucial work.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC detail/reasoning question on a mechanism explained in one sentence.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q123.** The passage explains the Matthew effect's compounding over time chiefly through:",
    "A) early credit bringing resources that enable more credit B) prize committees banning newcomers from all awards C) eminent researchers being paid to train new ones D) less-known collaborators losing interest in the field",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage explains the compounding directly: \"early credit brings the resources — funding, favoured students, faster publication — that make future high-profile discoveries more likely, which then earn still more credit.\" That chain — credit leads to resources leads to more credit — is option A exactly. Option B invents a ban on newcomers the passage never states. Option C invents a payment scheme never mentioned. Option D is wrong because the passage never says collaborators lose interest; it says they simply do not receive proportional credit, a different problem from losing interest.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC mechanism-tracing question inside a dense passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q124.** Which of the following claims does the passage explicitly avoid making?",
    "A) That eminent researchers are never genuinely skilled B) That fame functions as a proxy under scarce attention C) That credit can compound into a self-reinforcing loop D) That the effect is named after a biblical verse",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Look for what the author explicitly refuses to claim. The passage says the corrective \"is not to deny that eminent researchers are often genuinely skilled\" — meaning the author deliberately avoids claiming they are never skilled. That is option A. Options B, C and D are all things the passage DOES state plainly: B is stated (\"eminence functions as a signal under conditions of scarce attention\"), C is stated (the compounding loop), and D is stated (\"named... after the biblical verse\"). Since the question asks which claim the passage avoids making, and only option A is avoided while B, C and D are all directly asserted, A is the one correct answer.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC negative-scan question requiring a careful line-by-line check.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
