    "### 📄 RC — Commons, misremembered",
    "",
    "The textbook account of the enclosure movement runs simply enough: shared pastures, once open to any villager's livestock, were fenced by landowners into private plots, and a genuine commons collapsed into private property. The story is usually told as an unambiguous loss, and often invoked today to warn that anything left unowned will eventually be seized by whoever fences it first.",
    "",
    "The historical record complicates this in an inconvenient direction. Many of the pastures called commons were never open to all comers; they were governed by dense local rules — how many animals each household could graze, which weeks were reserved for hay, who could gather firewood and how much — enforced by manor courts long before any hedge was planted. What enclosure abolished, in a great many recorded cases, was not an absence of rules but a local rulebook, replacing it with a national one centred on individual title. Whether this counted as loss or gain depended entirely on whose interests the local rulebook had served: for households the old rules protected, enclosure was dispossession; for outsiders the local rules had long excluded, the change mattered little, since they had never held commons rights to begin with. The lesson is not that enclosure was benign, but that \"commons\" and \"unowned\" are not synonyms, and treating them as such flattens a history of genuine, if unequal, governance into a myth of an empty land.",
    "",
    "**Q125.** The passage's main point is best expressed as:",
    "A) Commons were governed, not simply unowned B) Enclosure was, on balance, clearly beneficial C) Manor courts had no power over pastures D) National title began only after enclosure",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage opens with the textbook story (commons = unowned, then fenced), then says the record \"complicates this in an inconvenient direction\": most commons were actually run by \"dense local rules\" enforced by manor courts, long before enclosure. So the passage's real point is that commons were governed, not empty of rules, which is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage's final line refuses to call enclosure simply beneficial, calling it dispossession for the households the old rules protected. Option C is wrong because the passage says manor courts enforced grazing limits \"long before any hedge was planted\", meaning they clearly did have power. Option D is wrong because national title is described as replacing local rules, not as a brand-new idea with no prior local equivalent.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC central-idea question on a layered economic-history passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q126.** The passage explicitly states that its point is NOT that:",
    "A) enclosure was benign B) commons had local rules C) manor courts enforced grazing limits D) \"commons\" and \"unowned\" differ",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Find the exact sentence: \"The lesson is not that enclosure was benign.\" This tells us directly, in the author's own words, what the passage refuses to conclude. That is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage clearly does state commons had local rules — that is practically the whole argument. Option C is wrong because the passage explicitly says manor courts enforced grazing limits. Option D is wrong because the passage's very last sentence explicitly argues that \"commons\" and \"unowned\" are not synonyms, meaning the passage does claim they differ — so this is something the passage affirms, not avoids.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC negative-scan question built around a direct quote.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q127.** According to the passage, enclosure mattered least to:",
    "A) outsiders whom the local rules had always excluded B) households the old local rules used to protect C) manor courts that had enforced the old rules D) landowners who newly fenced the pastures",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage says plainly: \"for outsiders the local rules had long excluded, the change mattered little, since they had never held commons rights to begin with.\" People who were never included by the old rules had nothing to lose when those rules were replaced, so the change barely affected them. That is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage says the exact opposite for these households: for them, enclosure \"was dispossession\", meaning it mattered a great deal. Option C is wrong because the passage never discusses how much the change mattered specifically to manor courts as an institution. Option D is wrong because landowners who gained fenced pastures were clearly major beneficiaries, so the change mattered greatly to them too, just in a positive direction, not \"least\".",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC inference question on who a historical change affected least.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q128.** Which finding would most challenge the passage's central claim about commons?",
    "A) Records showing many pastures had no grazing rules B) Records showing manor courts kept yearly minutes C) Evidence some enclosures raised productivity D) Evidence title emerged before the 1700s",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's core claim is that commons were governed by \"dense local rules\", not left empty of rules. If new records showed that many named commons pastures actually had no grazing rules of any kind, that would directly contradict this core claim, which is exactly option A. Option B is wrong because showing manor courts kept meeting minutes would, if anything, support the claim that governance existed, not challenge it. Option C is wrong because a claim about productivity says nothing about whether the pastures were ruled or ruleless before enclosure — it is simply a different question. Option D is wrong because a date for when national title emerged does not address whether local rule-governed commons existed before hedges were planted; it is a separate historical detail.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC weaken-the-argument question on a historical-claim passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
