    "### 📄 RC — The edited self",
    "",
    "Ask someone who they are, and sooner or later they will answer with a story: a sequence of remembered events arranged so that the person they were becomes, plausibly, the person they now are. Philosophers who study personal identity have grown suspicious of how much work this story is doing. Memory, the material from which the story is built, is not a recording retrieved intact but a reconstruction assembled fresh each time, borrowing details from the present to fill gaps in the past. Two people who lived through the same event will, months later, recall meaningfully different versions of it, each convinced their version is simply what happened.",
    "",
    "If memory is this unreliable, one might expect the self built from it to feel correspondingly fragile. It rarely does, and the reason is instructive rather than reassuring: narrative coherence is achieved not despite memory's editing but because of it. The mind quietly discards details that would make the story awkward and foregrounds those that make it hang together, and this editing is invisible from the inside, so the resulting self feels discovered rather than constructed. The philosophical worry, then, is not that identity is an illusion, but that its apparent solidity is itself evidence of how thoroughly, and undetectably, memory has been shaped to produce it.",
    "",
    "**Q129.** The passage's central claim is best stated as:",
    "A) A stable self feels solid because memory edits itself B) Memory retrieves past events like an intact recording C) Two witnesses of an event always recall it identically D) Philosophers agree personal identity is a total illusion",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's final sentence sums up its own point: the self's \"apparent solidity is itself evidence of how thoroughly... memory has been shaped to produce it.\" In simple words, the self feels solid partly BECAUSE memory quietly edits itself to make a smooth story. That is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage explicitly says memory is \"not a recording retrieved intact but a reconstruction\" — the opposite of an intact recording. Option C is wrong because the passage says two witnesses recall \"meaningfully different versions\", not identical ones. Option D is wrong because the passage explicitly says the worry is NOT that identity is an illusion; it is about how the solid feeling is produced.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC central-claim question on a dense cognitive-science/philosophy passage.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q130.** The passage explicitly denies that its worry is that:",
    "A) identity is an illusion B) memory reconstructs the past C) editing of memory is invisible D) coherence rewards a filtered story",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Find the exact sentence: \"the worry, then, is not that identity is an illusion.\" This is the author directly telling us what they are NOT arguing. That is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage clearly does claim memory reconstructs the past — that is stated as fact early on. Option C is wrong because the passage explicitly says the editing \"is invisible from the inside\", meaning the passage does claim this. Option D is wrong because the passage's whole final point is that coherence rewards, or comes from, a filtered story — this is exactly what the author does claim, not denies.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC negative-scan question built around a direct quote.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q131.** According to the passage, the self feels stable mainly because:",
    "A) memory discards awkward details, keeps fitting ones B) memory is, in fact, an accurate intact recording C) philosophers have proven the self does not exist D) two people always recall shared events identically",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage explains this directly: \"the mind quietly discards details that would make the story awkward and foregrounds those that make it hang together.\" In simple words, memory throws away the messy bits and keeps the bits that fit a neat story, which is why the story, and the self built from it, feels solid. That is option A. Option B is wrong because the passage says memory is a \"reconstruction\", explicitly not an intact recording. Option C is wrong because the passage never claims the self does not exist; it only questions how solid it really is. Option D is wrong because the passage earlier said two people recall the same event differently, not identically.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC mechanism question requiring a precise textual link.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q132.** Which scenario best illustrates the passage's account of memory shaping identity?",
    "A) A man recalls his youth as ambitious, forgetting drift B) A man keeps a diary and checks memories against it daily C) A scientist recovers a lost video of an old event D) Two strangers agree on every detail of a street",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** The passage's mechanism is: memory quietly drops awkward details and keeps details that make a coherent story, and this editing happens without the person noticing. The man in option A does precisely this — he remembers himself as steadily ambitious while forgetting years where he actually drifted, producing a neat, coherent story. Option B is wrong because actively cross-checking memories against a diary is a deliberate check AGAINST natural editing, not an example of it happening invisibly. Option C is about an external video recording, which is not memory at all, so it does not illustrate how internal memory reconstructs a self. Option D is about two strangers agreeing on a street's details, which has nothing to do with building a personal life-story from selectively edited memories.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — RC application question, the final and hardest question in the set.*",
