    "**Q133.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. Song-dynasty merchants, weary of hauling strings of heavy iron coins across provinces, began depositing coin with trusted shops in exchange for a paper receipt. 2. Local governments eventually noticed the convenience and issued their own standardized notes, redeemable at a fixed rate, well before any European state attempted the same. 3. These receipts, promising repayment on demand, started to circulate hand to hand as payment in their own right, long before any coin changed hands again. 4. Yet the system also carried a temptation no metal currency offered so easily: a government facing a shortfall could simply print more notes than it held coin to back. 5. Repeated over-issue eventually drained public confidence, and by the dynasty's later years merchants once again preferred the heavier, harder-to-fake weight of metal. 6. The episode is often cited as the world's first sustained experiment with paper money, complete with its first sustained bout of the inflation such money makes possible. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 1-3-2-4-5-6 B) 6-1-3-2-4-5 C) 1-2-3-4-5-6 D) 1-3-2-5-4-6",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Anchor 1: sentence 3 says \"These receipts\", which needs sentence 1 to have already introduced receipts, so 1 comes before 3. Anchor 2: sentence 2 says \"Local governments eventually noticed the convenience\", meaning receipts were already circulating as payment, which is what sentence 3 just described — so 3 comes before 2. Anchor 3: sentence 4 opens with \"Yet the system also carried a temptation\", which needs the government-run note system from sentence 2 to already exist, so 2 comes before 4. Anchor 4: sentence 5 describes the result of repeated over-issue, which is the temptation from sentence 4 acted upon again and again, so 4 comes before 5. Sentence 6 sums the whole episode up as a conclusion, so it comes last. Chain: 1-3-2-4-5-6, option A. Option B tempts by opening with sentence 6, but sentence 6 says \"the episode\", a phrase that only makes sense once we already know what episode is being discussed — it cannot open the paragraph. Option C swaps 2 and 3, but then sentence 2's \"convenience\" would have nothing to point back to yet. Option D swaps 4 and 5, but then sentence 5's \"repeated over-issue\" would come before the single act of over-issuing was even introduced in sentence 4.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — six-sentence para-jumble, TITA type, on a monetary-history theme.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q134.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. A merchant recording only what he received made money vanish from view the moment it left his hands, with no ledger line to show where it had gone. 2. Double-entry bookkeeping, formalised by an Italian friar in 1494 though practised by merchants for at least two centuries before him, solved this by recording every transaction twice. 3. Each entry appeared once as a debit in one account and once as a credit in another, so the books could never silently lose track of a coin. 4. Medieval merchants tracking growing, far-flung trade needed a way to know, at a glance, whether a venture had gained or lost money overall. 5. If the two sides ever failed to balance, the discrepancy itself pointed straight to the page on which an error had been made. 6. Older single-entry records, little more than a diary of cash in and cash out, could not supply that overall picture on their own. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 4-6-1-2-3-5 B) 1-6-4-2-3-5 C) 4-1-6-2-3-5 D) 4-6-2-1-3-5",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Anchor 1: sentence 6 says \"that overall picture\", which can only refer back to the specific need named in sentence 4 (\"know, at a glance, whether a venture had gained or lost money\"), so 4 comes before 6. Anchor 2: sentence 1 then elaborates the exact problem with old single-entry books, money vanishing from view, continuing straight from sentence 6's complaint. Anchor 3: sentence 2 says \"solved this\", where \"this\" is the vanishing-money problem from sentence 1, so 1 comes before 2. Anchor 4: sentence 3 explains exactly how double-entry works, directly unpacking sentence 2's claim that transactions are recorded twice, so 2 comes before 3. Anchor 5: sentence 5 describes a further benefit that builds on the mechanism just explained in sentence 3, so it comes last. Chain: 4-6-1-2-3-5, option A. Option B opens with sentence 1 instead of 4, but sentence 6's \"that overall picture\" has no clear meaning unless sentence 4 has already named the picture being sought. Option C swaps 6 and 1, breaking the same link. Option D swaps 1 and 2, but sentence 2's \"solved this\" would then have nothing specific to solve yet.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 VARC — six-sentence para-jumble, TITA type, on a history-of-accounting theme.*",
