    "**Q135.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. A fisherman who leaves a school of cod uncaught gains nothing personally, since any fish he spares today may simply be caught by a rival boat tomorrow. 2. Each individual boat, behaving with impeccable local logic, can still drive the shared stock toward collapse, since no one boat's restraint is rewarded. 3. This mismatch between individual incentive and collective outcome is the central puzzle economists call the tragedy of the commons. 4. Some fishing communities have nonetheless avoided collapse for centuries, through locally enforced rules restricting who may fish, when, and how much. 5. Such arrangements work only where the community can exclude outsiders and monitor its own members, conditions that break down once a resource crosses borders or open ocean. 6. Where those conditions hold, a commons can be managed sustainably without any need for either private ownership or a central regulator. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 1-2-3-4-5-6 B) 3-1-2-4-5-6 C) 1-2-3-5-4-6 D) 1-3-2-4-5-6",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Anchor 1: sentence 2 continues the exact logic sentence 1 started — one boat gains nothing by sparing fish because rivals will just catch them, so 1 comes before 2. Anchor 2: sentence 3 says \"This mismatch\", which needs the individual-versus-collective problem from sentences 1 and 2 to already be laid out, so 3 cannot open the paragraph even though it reads like a general definition. Anchor 3: sentence 4 begins with \"nonetheless\", signalling a contrast with the bleak picture just painted in sentence 3, so 3 comes before 4. Anchor 4: sentence 5 explains the exact conditions behind the \"locally enforced rules\" just introduced in sentence 4, so 4 comes before 5. Anchor 5: sentence 6 then draws the conclusion from those same conditions, so it comes last. Chain: 1-2-3-4-5-6, option A. Option B tempts by opening with sentence 3, since it reads like a textbook definition, but \"this mismatch\" is a phrase that needs sentences 1 and 2 to come first. Option C swaps sentences 4 and 5, but then sentence 5's \"such arrangements\" would have nothing specific to point back to yet. Option D swaps 2 and 3, breaking the link between the mismatch and its name.",
