    "**Q136.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. A penny bought not just a cup of coffee in seventeenth-century London but a seat at a table where strangers debated news, politics and rumour on equal footing. 2. Historians have since argued that these rooms did something no royal court or church pulpit had managed: they let opinion form outside the control of any single authority. 3. That equal footing was narrower than it looked: a coffeehouse seated a lord beside a clerk, provided both could pay the entrance penny, but seated no woman as a customer at all. 4. That opinion, once formed, proved difficult for the crown to suppress, since it now existed simultaneously in hundreds of independent rooms rather than one traceable source. 5. Historians disagree, however, on how much of this reflects a real broadening of who held influence versus a reshuffling among men who already had it. 6. Governments periodically tried closing the coffeehouses outright, an overreaction that only confirmed how seriously the new talk was taken. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
