    "**Correct: A)** Anchor 1: sentence 3 says \"That equal footing\", a phrase that can only follow sentence 1, the only sentence mentioning people meeting \"on equal footing\" — so 1 must lead straight into 3. Anchor 2: sentence 2 says \"these rooms\", referring to the coffeehouses already described, and it opens the historians' bigger argument about opinion forming outside any single authority. Anchor 3: sentence 4 says \"That opinion, once formed\", which needs sentence 2's \"opinion form\" immediately before it; if sentence 3 were placed between 2 and 4, this link would be awkwardly broken by an unrelated aside about gender exclusion, so 2 must lead straight into 4. Anchor 4: sentence 6 continues the same thread, describing governments' reaction once opinion proved hard to suppress. Anchor 5: sentence 5 closes the paragraph with a qualifying \"however\", pulling back from the optimistic claim in sentence 2. Chain: 1-3-2-4-6-5, option A. Option B breaks anchor 1 by inserting sentence 2 between sentences 1 and 3, stranding \"that equal footing\" further from its only possible referent. Option C swaps 4 and 6. Option D moves sentence 5 too early, before the argument it is meant to qualify has even been fully laid out.",
