    "**Q137.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. Before the 1880s, most towns kept their own local time, set by the sun overhead, so noon in one city could be several minutes off from noon forty miles away. 2. Railway companies found this unworkable once trains began crossing dozens of such towns in a single day, each keeping a slightly different clock. 3. Timetables printed for passengers had to list two, sometimes three, competing local times for the very same train at the very same moment. 4. Confusion of this kind eventually convinced companies to adopt a small number of standard time zones, sacrificing solar accuracy for shared punctuality. 5. Ordinary citizens resisted the change at first, since it meant a town clock could now read noon while the sun stood plainly on one side of the sky. 6. Within a generation, however, the convenience of a single predictable schedule outweighed the discomfort of a clock no longer tied to the sun above. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
    "A) 1-2-3-4-5-6 B) 1-3-2-4-5-6 C) 1-2-4-3-5-6 D) 2-1-3-4-5-6",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: A)** Anchor 1: sentence 2 says \"this\" is unworkable, referring to the local-time variation just described in sentence 1, so 1 comes before 2. Anchor 2: sentence 3 elaborates exactly how that unworkability showed up in printed timetables, continuing straight from sentence 2. Anchor 3: sentence 4 says \"confusion of this kind\", pointing back to the multiple competing times just described in sentence 3, so 3 comes before 4. Anchor 4: sentence 5 says \"the change\", referring to the standard time zones just adopted in sentence 4, so 4 comes before 5. Anchor 5: sentence 6 opens with \"however\", directly answering the resistance just described in sentence 5. Chain: 1-2-3-4-5-6, option A. Option B swaps 2 and 3, but then sentence 3's specific timetable example would come before the general unworkability it is meant to illustrate. Option C swaps 4 and 5, leaving \"the change\" in sentence 5 with nothing to refer to yet. Option D swaps 1 and 2, but sentence 2's opening word \"this\" would then have no local-time variation to point back to.",
