    "**Q138.** The six sentences below, labelled 1 to 6, make a coherent paragraph when properly sequenced. Choose the correct order. 1. For much of the twentieth century, doctors treated nostalgia as a genuine illness, a homesick soldier's longing for his village diagnosed alongside fevers and wounds. 2. That clinical suspicion has reversed almost entirely: controlled studies now link nostalgic recall to higher reported mood, stronger social belonging, and even a measurable rise in tolerance for physical cold. 3. Nostalgia, on this newer view, functions less like a disorder and more like a psychological thermostat, activated precisely when belonging feels threatened. 4. Subjects made to feel briefly lonely in an experiment, for instance, spontaneously recall nostalgic memories at a noticeably higher rate than subjects who feel secure. 5. The thermostat metaphor also explains why nostalgia clusters around threshold moments — graduations, migrations, break-ups — when a settled sense of self is precisely what is in question. 6. Even the word's origin, coined from the Greek for homecoming and pain, hints at how long this in-between status has clung to the idea. *(TITA in the actual exam)*",
