    "### 🧩 Cricket and football at Greenfield School",
    "",
    "In a class of 30 students, 18 play cricket and 15 play football. Of these, 8 students play both cricket and football. Every student either plays one of the two sports, both, or neither.",
    "",
    "**Q17.** How many students play only cricket (cricket but not football)?",
    "A) 8 B) 10 C) 15 D) 18",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** \"Only cricket\" means students who play cricket but exclude the ones who also play football. Total cricket players = 18. Of these, 8 also play football, so those 8 must be removed from the cricket-only count. So only-cricket = 18 − 8 = **10**. Option D (18) forgets to remove the overlap; option A (8) is actually the both-sports count, not the only-cricket count. Drawing a small two-circle picture with the overlap written first always prevents this mix-up.",
    "",
    "[[VIZ]]{\"type\":\"venn2\",\"title\":\"Cricket vs football players\",\"labels\":[\"Cricket\",\"Football\"],\"counts\":{\"onlyA\":10,\"onlyB\":7,\"both\":8,\"none\":5}}[[/VIZ]]",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — two-set Venn-style overlap question, a common easy LR warm-up.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q18.** How many students play neither cricket nor football?",
    "A) 3 B) 5 C) 7 D) 10",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** First find how many play at least one sport. Only-cricket = 18 − 8 = 10 (from Q17). Only-football = 15 − 8 = 7. Both = 8. Add all three non-overlapping groups: 10 + 7 + 8 = 25 students play at least one sport. The class has 30 students in total, so the number playing neither is 30 − 25 = **5**. Option D (10) mistakes the only-cricket count for the \"neither\" count; option C (7) does the same with only-football. Always build the \"at least one\" total first, then subtract from the class size.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — complement (neither/none) question inside a two-set overlap scenario.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q19.** How many students play at least one of the two sports?",
    "A) 15 B) 18 C) 23 D) 25",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: D)** \"At least one\" means only-cricket plus only-football plus both. From Q17 and Q18's working: only-cricket = 10, only-football = 7, both = 8. Add them: 10 + 7 + 8 = **25**. Alternatively, use the direct formula: cricket + football − both = 18 + 15 − 8 = 33 − 8 = 25, the same answer. Option B (18) is just the cricket total alone, ignoring football entirely; option C (23) comes from forgetting to add the both-sports group back in.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — union-of-two-sets question, testing the addition/subtraction overlap rule.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q20.** How many students play exactly one of the two sports (not both)?",
    "A) 15 B) 17 C) 18 D) 25",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: B)** \"Exactly one\" excludes the students who play both. Only-cricket = 10 and only-football = 7 (from Q17–Q18). Add these two only-groups together: 10 + 7 = **17**. Option D (25) is the at-least-one total from Q19, which wrongly includes the 8 who play both; option C (18) mistakes the raw cricket total for this count. The phrase \"exactly one\" is a strong signal to add the two only-groups and stop there, without touching the both-group.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — exactly-one-of-two-sets question, a classic Venn-diagram closer.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
