    "### 🧩 Three generations, six ages",
    "",
    "In the Verma family, three generations live together: grandparents Grandfather (GF) and Grandmother (GM); parents Father (F) and Mother (M); and children Son (S) and Daughter (D). All six ages are whole numbers of years. The following is known:",
    "",
    "1. Grandfather is 4 years older than Grandmother, and their two ages add up to 132.",
    "2. Grandfather's age is 12 less than twice Father's age.",
    "3. Father is 4 years older than Mother.",
    "4. Son and Daughter's ages add up to 24, and they differ from each other by 4 years.",
    "5. If Son is older than Daughter, then Daughter's age would have to be a prime number.",
    "",
    "**Q137.** What is Grandfather's age?",
    "A) 64 B) 66 C) 68 D) 70",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Step 1: clue 1 gives two facts about GF and GM together: GF − GM = 4 (GF older by 4) and GF + GM = 132 (their sum). Step 2: add these two equations directly: (GF − GM) + (GF + GM) = 4 + 132, which simplifies to 2×GF = 136. Step 3: divide by 2: GF = **68**. (Then GM = 68 − 4 = 64, and checking: 68 + 64 = 132 ✓.)",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — sum-and-difference age puzzle solved by adding two linear equations, a classic algebra-in-LR technique.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q138.** What is Daughter's age?",
    "A) 10 B) 12 C) 14 D) 16",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Step 1: clue 4 says Son + Daughter = 24 and they differ by 4. Two possibilities fit these two facts: (Son=14, Daughter=10) or (Son=10, Daughter=14). Step 2: clue 5 says IF Son is older than Daughter, THEN Daughter's age must be prime. Test the first possibility (Son=14 > Daughter=10): this would require Daughter's age, 10, to be prime — but 10 = 2 × 5, so it is NOT prime. That breaks clue 5, so this possibility is impossible. Step 3: the only option left is Son=10, Daughter=14 (Daughter older than Son) — here clue 5's 'if' condition (Son older) is simply not triggered, so there's nothing to check, and no contradiction arises. So Daughter is **14**.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — sum-and-difference puzzle with a conditional clue resolved by testing the forbidden branch for a contradiction (mirrors the real exam's habit of hiding one extra logical step inside an otherwise arithmetic set).*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q139.** What is the combined age of Father and Mother?",
    "A) 72 B) 74 C) 76 D) 78",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Step 1: from Q137, Grandfather's age is 68. Clue 2 says GF = 2×F − 12, so 68 = 2F − 12. Step 2: add 12 to both sides: 2F = 80. Step 3: divide by 2: F = **40**. Step 4: clue 3 says F − M = 4, so M = 40 − 4 = **36**. Step 5: combined age = F + M = 40 + 36 = **76**.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — chained linear-equation solving across two generations of an age puzzle.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q140.** What is the total combined age of all six family members?",
    "A) 228 B) 230 C) 232 D) 234",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Collecting every age solved so far: GF = 68, GM = 64, F = 40, M = 36, S = 10, D = 14. Add them in pairs to keep the arithmetic manageable: (GF + GM) = 132, (F + M) = 76 (from Q139), and (S + D) = 24 (given directly in clue 4). Total = 132 + 76 + 24 = 208 + 24 = **232**.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — grand-total check across a fully solved family-age puzzle, rewarding solvers who reuse already-computed sub-sums instead of re-adding six numbers from scratch.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "### 🧩 Choosing the four-member committee",
    "",
    "A society must select exactly 4 of its 7 members — Anil, Bela, Chirag, Deepa, Eshan, Farida and Gopal — to form a committee. The following conditions govern the selection:",
    "",
    "1. If Anil is selected, then Bela must also be selected.",
    "2. Chirag and Deepa cannot both be selected.",
    "3. If Eshan is not selected, then Farida must be selected.",
    "4. Gopal is selected only if Chirag is also selected.",
    "5. At least one of Anil or Eshan must be selected.",
    "6. Exactly two of Bela, Deepa and Farida are selected.",
    "7. Chirag is not selected.",
    "8. If Deepa is selected, then Chirag must also be selected.",
    "",
    "**Q141.** Which of the following candidates is selected for the committee?",
    "A) Chirag B) Deepa C) Farida D) Gopal",
    "<details><summary>Answer & explanation</summary>",
    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Step 1: clue 7 directly tells us Chirag is NOT selected. Step 2: clue 8 says if Deepa were selected, Chirag would have to be selected too — but Chirag is out (Step 1), so Deepa cannot be selected either (otherwise clue 8 would be broken). Step 3: clue 4 says Gopal is selected only if Chirag is — since Chirag is out, Gopal is out too, by the same reasoning. Step 4: that rules out 3 of the 7 members (Chirag, Deepa, Gopal), leaving exactly 4 eligible people: Anil, Bela, Eshan, Farida. Since the committee needs exactly 4 members and only 4 remain eligible, ALL FOUR of them must be selected — there's no choice left. Quick check against the remaining clues: clue 1 (Anil ⇒ Bela) holds since both are in; clue 3 (Eshan out ⇒ Farida in) is irrelevant since Eshan is in; clue 5 (Anil or Eshan) holds; clue 6 (exactly two of Bela/Deepa/Farida) holds since Bela and Farida are in, Deepa is out — that's exactly 2. Everything checks out. So **Farida** (option C) is indeed selected, while Chirag, Deepa and Gopal are all excluded.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 DILR — selection/committee set solved by a double contrapositive elimination followed by a headcount-forced final step.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
    "",
    "**Q142.** How many of the seven candidates are NOT selected for the committee?",
    "A) 2 B) 3 C) 4 D) 5",
