    "",
    "**Correct: C)** Step 1: since PA and PB are tangents, the radius to each touching point is perpendicular (at 90°) to that tangent — so angle OAP = 90° and angle OBP = 90°. Step 2: the four points O, A, P, B form a quadrilateral (a four-sided figure) if we connect them in order O→A→P→B→O. Step 3: a basic geometry fact is that the four interior angles of any quadrilateral always add up to 360°. Step 4: the four angles of quadrilateral OAPB are: angle at O (= angle AOB, what we want), angle at A (= 90°, from Step 1), angle at P (= angle APB = 50°, given), and angle at B (= 90°, from Step 1). Step 5: so angle AOB + 90° + 50° + 90° = 360°. Step 6: combine the known angles: 90 + 50 + 90 = 230. So angle AOB = 360 − 230 = **130°**. Step 7: sanity check — as angle APB gets smaller (tangents nearly parallel, point P very far away), angle AOB should get closer to 180°, and indeed a smaller APB (say 10°) would give AOB = 360−90−90−10=170°, closer to 180°, confirming the inverse relationship makes sense. Option A (110°) and option D (140°) come from arithmetic slips in the 360° subtraction.",
    "",
    "📌 *Modeled on: CAT 2025 QA — the tangent-pair quadrilateral angle relation, from the circles topic area.*",
    "",
    "</details>",
